[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end connectivity and return routing across the intercompany link and found no evidence of replies being lost. From pc1 to pc2 and back, ICMP probes succeed with 0% loss and low RTTs. iperf throughput tests confirm sustained bidirectional data flow (≈120–137 Mbps), indicating that return traffic from pc2 reaches pc1 reliably. Path MTU is healthy: pc1 can ping pc2 with 1500-byte frames (1472 payload, DF set) without fragmentation issues, so large-packet replies are not being blackholed. 

Routing is correct on both routers: router1 advertises 195.11.14.0/24 and learns 200.1.1.0/24 via BGP; router2 does the reverse. Each router’s RIB/FIB shows the proper next-hop over 193.10.11.0/24, and BGP sessions are established and stable. Interface queue stats (tc) on all router interfaces show zero drops/overlimits, suggesting no queuing loss on the path.

Localization: All devices (router1 eth0/eth1, router2 eth0/eth1, pc1, pc2) appear to be operating normally. There is no misconfiguration or failing daemon observed in the lab at this time.

Root cause: Not reproducible; the network path is currently healthy, so earlier symptoms may have been transient or endpoint-related.

Corrective fix: None required on the network. If the issue recurs, capture packets on pc1 and pc2 and check host firewalls or application settings; confirm correct destination IP and test during the incident to pinpoint any intermittent host-side blocking. 
=== END ===